~ _play_glitch_memory_combat
Kaelen (Internal): My hands are shaking. Not from fear — from RECOGNITION. They remember something my brain doesn't. A fighting stance? When did I ever...
System: // MEMORY FRAGMENT DETECTED — Corrupted combat data leaking from consciousness buffer\n// Loading partial combat subroutine...
System: // GLITCH MEMORY: A corrupted fragment. Something you once knew.\n// [J] Attack  |  [K] Parry  |  [A/D] Move
System: // MEMORY FRAGMENT COMPLETE — Combat reflexes partially restored\n// +25 XP
Kaelen (Internal): I... destroyed it. My body moved on its own. These reflexes — they're not from my life as a programmer. They're from HERE. From whatever this world is making me into.
System: // MEMORY FRAGMENT TIMED OUT — Combat data incomplete\n// Partial restoration: +10 XP
Kaelen (Internal): The memory is fading. But my hands still tingle. Whatever that was... I'll need it again.

~ create_awakening_cutscene
Kaelen (Internal): ...My head. Feels like someone scooped out my brain, shook it, and put it back in sideways.
Kaelen (Internal): Grass. Trees. Sky. The air smells like... ozone and wildflowers? That can't be right. I was on a plane. Flight 707. Tokyo to San Francisco.
Kaelen (Internal): I try to stand. My legs buckle immediately. My body feels wrong — lighter somehow, like I weigh less than I should. I hit the grass hard, palms first. It's wet. Cold. Real.
Kaelen (Internal): This is a dream. Obviously. Hypoxia from cabin depressurization, or my brain misfiring in the milliseconds before impact. The mind constructs elaborate scenarios to shield itself from death. I read that somewhere.
Kaelen: *He bites the inside of his cheek. Hard. The taste of copper floods his mouth. The pain is sharp and immediate and absolutely, undeniably real.*
Kaelen (Internal): ...Not a dream. Dreams don't hurt like this. I can feel individual blades of grass between my fingers. I can smell dirt and something sweet and chemical, like overheated electronics.
Kaelen (Internal): The plane. The passengers. The sky TEARING ITSELF APART. The screens glitching. System errors everywhere. A blinding white flash. That wasn't a dream either. So where am I? What happened to me?
Kaelen (Internal): Breathe, Kaelen. Catalogue. When you can't understand something, reduce it to components. That's what you do. That's what you've ALWAYS done. So do it now, before the panic swallows you.
Kaelen: The ground doesn't line up. Look — there's a gap between the hillside and the path, like someone built them separately and just pushed them together without checking if they fit.
Kaelen (Internal): That tree. It's floating three meters off the ground. And its shadow falls the wrong way — the shadow points east while the sun is in the west. Like two different people built the tree and the lighting and never talked to each other.
Kaelen: *He reaches out and taps the invisible boundary around the floating tree. His hand stops against nothing — an invisible collision box.*
Kaelen: An invisible wall. My hand stops against thin air. The tree isn't floating — the ground just doesn't reach it. Like whoever made this place forgot to connect the pieces.
Kaelen (Internal): And there — that stream. It's flowing UPHILL. Water doesn't do that. Not in any world that makes sense. Nobody checked this. Nobody tested any of this.
Kaelen: No magic circles. No deities descending from the clouds. No mysterious old man with a quest scroll.
Kaelen: Just a half-finished world that someone built in a hurry and never bothered to polish.
Kaelen: I'm not in a fantasy world. I'm inside a broken machine.
Kaelen (Internal): I check my hands. Five fingers on each — good, at least my body seems complete. I'm wearing clothes I don't recognize. Some kind of generic adventurer outfit. Leather and cloth. Like someone dressed me for a role I didn't audition for.
Kaelen (Internal): The plane. Flight 707. I remember the turbulence. I remember the screens flickering. I remember system error messages flooding my vision and then... nothing but white.
Kaelen (Internal): AetherCorp's Project Aethelgard. The one I spent three years building. The one with the anomalous code patterns. I'm INSIDE it.
Kaelen (Internal): Which means the plane... the other passengers... Mira, the kid with the GameBoy, the businessman with his spreadsheets... What happened to them? Are they here too? Are they ALIVE?
Kaelen (Internal): Am I dead? Is this what death looks like for someone who spent their whole life staring at screens — an eternity trapped inside one?
Kaelen: *He sits in the grass for a long time. The stream gurgles uphill. A bird sings the same four notes over and over, never changing. He lets the weight of it all settle on his shoulders.*
Kaelen (Internal): I can't fall apart here. If there's even a chance those people are somewhere in this system — if there's even a chance this ISN'T death — then sitting in a crater feeling sorry for myself helps no one. Least of all me.
Kaelen: Alright. Think. If I'm inside some kind of constructed world, there are rules. Patterns. Weaknesses I can find and use. I need to find a way out. And if there are other survivors... I need to find them first.
System: [LOCATION: SECTOR_0 — CRASH_SITE]\n[USER STATUS: Disoriented but functional]\n[ROOT ACCESS: Detected — Level 1]\n[OBJECTIVE: EXIT CRATER. FOLLOW GOLDEN_PATH.]
Kaelen (Internal): A golden path. A literal glowing trail on the ground, leading somewhere. Could it be more obvious? Whoever built this world really wants me to go this way.
Kaelen: Fine. Let's follow the glowing breadcrumbs and see where this world wants me to go.
